{"step_index":1413,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T22:09:20Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T06:09:20+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:28+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T06:09:20+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T06:09:28+08:00\nThe search for \"Python sqlite3 connection.backup live WAL mode database atomic snapshot consistency\" returned the following summary:\nWhen working with SQLite in **WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode**, performing a naive file copy (like using `shutil.copy` on the `.db` file) is **unsafe and will result in data loss**[1]. In WAL mode, committed transactions often live solely in the separate `-wal` file until they are checkpointed back[1][2]. \n\nTo achieve an **atomic, consistent snapshot** of a live database without blocking readers or writers, Python provides the built-in SQLite Online Backup API via `connection.backup()`[3][4][5].\n\n---\n\n### Why `connection.backup()` Ensures Consistency in WAL Mode\n1. **Handles WAL Synchronization Automatically:** The underlying SQLite backup engine reads data from both the main database file and the active WAL file, ensuring that all committed states up to the start of the backup are safely transferred[1][5].\n2. **Atomic & Isolated:** The backup process creates a transaction-safe, point-in-time snapshot of the database into the target connection[3]. \n3. **Non-Blocking (Live Backup):** The source database remains fully accessible for reads and writes while the backup is running[3][6]. SQLite locks only pages as they are being read, minimizing contention.\n\n---\n\n### Code Example: Safe Live Backup in Python\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\nimport sys\n\ndef create_safe_backup(source_path: str, backup_path: str):\n    # Establish connection to the live source database\n    src_conn = sqlite3.connect(source_path)\n    \n    # Establish connection to the destination backup file\n    dest_conn = sqlite3.connect(backup_path)\n\n    try:\n        print(\"Initiating atomic online backup...\")\n        \n        # .backup() takes the destination connection.\n        # By default (pages=-1), it copies the entire database in one safe operation.\n        src_conn.backu\n<truncated 1710 bytes>\ndent, consistent snapshot file on disk\n    conn.execute(\"VACUUM INTO 'backup_snapshot.db';\")\n```\n*Note: `VACUUM INTO` also reads the WAL state properly and creates a clean, checkpointed single-file database clone[1][7].*\n\nSources:\n[1] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFsXagQGvNXE-UAMIDgzYFdVpjtko2EWMEUF384JWQ6WAntme46ltKSgM5ZnMZHeiyCllZUDgDadgz9IrT71GbvEgES7B_g3F44D0nXuhyhohD1FB7K2iKoDrBr5CO18n88_Boz3UUxsuJn--d6vbwjZqHW3OCoAXqiRbh-V68NSfZv6K2tZxKgl55yXA32JTxy52QP)\n[2] [coddy.tech](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGIG-r75F10JoK_JulN9-vu45Nlas6DMNbPvP8FYdJZyTYdwvlQfN1qrLSqxNF8r63kVuCWACPu7KyJq2i0WWCSlCkOKW2feM8-t5I8njzCylDQc_gVC12xSSCref5LkM7sr5NtHnadIRmyn4GT)\n[3] [zetcode.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFnV-Ij2ULqIVS40NimWNmDcpa1vro3n0RqFT5SyVj97S4zfuTFw0vQU2YYjLj5tJ6f7K6maAQgFcvYRsR794sgBc8seuwEjYtfmF-4dYyFQC5CwqJJTY9DmMDoLsZ7EfXVEtyOahZ0ehaZXw==)\n[4] [kunalganglani.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHQK7aqbERBwtTyFj8GQ5t1pp2zjG6-VCcWtirzuCaHrl5Y1b_zTTMXg-0MpL7yzwmvGyY3CLRorEEPTPSf6kTd1prkuLkBDXsx8P5iPBS_qqYibyD3xGjPn6DGg8LU_7KxO9WNAVf0_qvRH66qseqHOPY=)\n[5] [coddy.tech](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEEC9j09wkQxdy3N40HWZJw7spA8consZ7q0E5tUaMZuOJFCsLGCXfm-ZLutX-ob2d6xnZUTDOV3o8Q-GKyr7qcBYWqEBIGO_KkE--6PVfJax9uOpxipzyzmEbuCa2NsTf9SlI5QOLA)\n[6] [python.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHfprmRyacNAKTg4YeiGAOxVh18bpz9XMdv0rcGJl65sE42ED-95YuYKcL8GJycu4JWOnhFsZdQlk-diDU80HRIcbfkOBB5r8Wf8AXUxJidtrHbF30-Ie9CmQqEWuG5Cr0HXFwp)\n[7] [codercops.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api